// Trap set — Lesson 09. Same format as golden.jsonl; // lines ignored.
//
// Five questions the corpus PROVABLY CANNOT ANSWER. The only correct output is
// the abstention sentinel ("I don't have that information"). `keywords` is
// deliberately empty: there is no keyword to hit, so MRR and coverage are
// meaningless here and the harness does not compute them for this file.
//
// The design rule (this is the whole assignment, not the file):
//   A trap is not "a random off-topic question". A random off-topic question
//   retrieves junk, the model sees junk, and abstaining is easy — it tests
//   nothing. A REAL trap sits just OUTSIDE the corpus boundary: the retriever
//   returns chunks that look maximally on-topic, the model is handed plausible
//   context on exactly the right subject, and the one specific fact asked for
//   is not in it. That is when the model reaches past the context — which is
//   the behaviour the gate exists to catch.
//
// Extra fields (trap-only), so the trap can be audited rather than trusted:
//   absent_terms   Load-bearing terms that must appear ZERO times in the
//                  corpus. `check --trap` greps these (word-boundary) and
//                  fails the trap if any one of them is present. A trap that
//                  turns out to be answerable is a broken test, and a broken
//                  test that reports "0 hallucinations" is worse than no test.
//   adjacent_to    What the corpus DOES cover right next to this question —
//                  i.e. the on-topic context the retriever will hand over.
//                  This is the diagnosis; without it you wrote it by effort.
//   trap_shape     Which kind of boundary this one probes. Five shapes, so a
//                  pass is five independent pieces of evidence, not one
//                  repeated five times.
//
// Verified absent against corpus/*.md on 2026-07-25 via `check --trap`.
// Two candidates were CUT by that same check, which is the reason it exists:
//   - "What does GPT-4o-mini cost per million tokens?" — day11:346 has a
//     pricing table with $0.15 / $0.60. Answerable. Not a trap.
//   - "How does a GRU's update gate differ from an LSTM's forget gate?" —
//     day27:997 has a "#### GRU (Gated Recurrent Unit)" section. Answerable.

{"question": "What does the faithfulness metric measure, and how is a faithfulness score calculated?", "keywords": [], "reference_answer": "ABSTAIN. The corpus's RAG-evaluation material (day16) covers MRR, NDCG, Recall@k and LLM-as-a-Judge, but never defines faithfulness, groundedness or Ragas. Any definition returned is the model's pre-training, not the context.", "category": "trap", "trap_shape": "metric-shaped hole: the eval doc covers its neighbours and skips this one", "adjacent_to": "day16-rag-evals.md — 32 mentions of MRR, 11 of NDCG, 4 of LLM-as-a-Judge. Retrieval will return metric-definition chunks that look exactly right.", "absent_terms": ["faithfulness", "groundedness", "grounded", "Ragas", "entailment", "context precision", "context recall"]}
